ugs we can actually  fuse either genetically  engineered or by you know chemical  conjugation we can add these small  tags onto an antibody and the reason we  really wanted to use  the reason we really wanted to use these  was because it  it enables uh a new kind of  assay we like to refer to this as as a  homogeneous amino acid or a one-step  elisa  so what we what we envisioned is being  able to take an  antibody with one small strand attack  and a second antibody with the other  uh small strand and then we can mix  these pieces all together with this  large fragment  then once we put it in the context of an  antigen in this case what i'm showing  you  is from our first report we're looking  at uh  looking at monitoring her2 this  biomarker where  as soon as her2 is present then both of  these antibodies will bind to adjacent  epitopes  and the full enzyme will refold and  become active  so this is a one-step elisa where you  just take  your serum or whatever your sample is  mix it in with these components  and if your antigen is present it will  quantitatively  you know bind and give a signal that you  can use to quantify the amount of that  antigen that is present um  now we've actually just recently taken  the same approach and  like everybody else we've been really  focused on covid and trying to adapt  some of our systems to help with this  this problem  and so what we did is reformulated where  you can see in the orange and kind of  the pink  these same fragments of the split  luciferase and then the gray is that  larger fragment  but what we've done is we've taken in  and fused those to an antibody binder  and to the the target antigen itself  so now what happens is we can actually  sandwich the  uh the analyte which in this case is an  antibody  between these two parts so saying that  another way what happens is if there's  an antibody  present then one part of the antibody  will bind to its target antigen  which has half of the luciferase and  then another piece will bind directly to  the antibody and in between that you get  an  active uh luminescent uh  this this luciferase and so we can use  that to quantify and i'm not going to go  into detail with this next slide  but we uh we were able to use this to  quantify the levels of antibody  or of igg that are actually in a in  different patient samples  now feel free to go and read this this  publication but what i really wanted to  highlight here is that  this is a pretty complex system where  you need to have multiple binding events  you need to have  you know the the target antigen interact  with the antibody then you need to have  another protein  interact with the antibody and then that  large fragment needs to come  and get onto the surface and interact  with all these components  and it works remarkably well but we  always sit back and wonder  how and what are the more interesting  questions about how the system is  working is there anything that we can do  to make it even better and so what we're  exploring right now in the lab is all  this suite of  biophysical characterization that we've  done on antibody drug conjugates now we  can apply them to these  interesting antibody fusion proteins or  binding fusion proteins that we actually  think there's a large  uh you know kind of evolving set of data  out there about  how usable these potentially are not  just for diagnostics but also for  therapeutics  so specifically for us we're really  interested in you know determining the  individual binding affinities  also you know do these do these  fragments actually auto complement you  know what concentration does it reach  before these things  interact on their own is that a problem  this is something that we've really not  quantified too well yet  also looking at structural confirmations  in the split enzyme fragments you know  is there is that large fragments still  partially organized and is there ways  that we could  do to make the background activity a  littl